1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:05,400
Before this video goes any further,
I want you to try something.

2
00:00:06,230 --> 00:00:08,450
Think of the person who knows you best.

3
00:00:09,270 --> 00:00:10,500
Can you picture their face?

4
00:00:10,710 --> 00:00:15,560
Not just remember what they look like—can
you actually see a face in your mind?

5
00:00:16,150 --> 00:00:17,500
Now try something different.

6
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:21,390
Silently say, “I need to
remember this tomorrow.”

7
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:24,570
Did those words sound like
a voice inside your head?

8
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:28,290
Or did you understand the
sentence without hearing anything?

9
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:30,070
There are no right answers.

10
00:00:31,340 --> 00:00:34,090
And these little tests
cannot diagnose anything.

11
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:38,971
But they point to something strange: two
people can live in the same world and have

12
00:00:39,011 --> 00:00:42,090
very different experiences
inside their own minds.

13
00:00:42,530 --> 00:00:44,310
Some people see vivid pictures.

14
00:00:44,990 --> 00:00:46,240
Some see none.

15
00:00:46,730 --> 00:00:50,680
Some experience an almost
constant stream of inner words.

16
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:52,870
Others report very little.

17
00:00:53,740 --> 00:00:56,444
If you’ve always assumed
everybody thinks like you,

18
00:00:56,484 --> 00:01:00,170
some of the answers in this video
may sound almost impossible.

19
00:01:00,730 --> 00:01:04,620
The strange thing is, people on
both sides often feel that way.

20
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,536
And here’s the question: if our
minds can feel so different,

21
00:01:08,576 --> 00:01:11,370
how do we know what thinking actually is?

22
00:01:15,690 --> 00:01:19,940
In 2010, doctors described
a man they called MX.

23
00:01:20,050 --> 00:01:25,410
He was sixty-five, and something unusual
had happened after a heart procedure.

24
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:29,260
Before the procedure, he could
picture things in his mind.

25
00:01:29,970 --> 00:01:31,820
Afterwards, he couldn’t.

26
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:36,064
He could still recognize objects
and remember facts about them,

27
00:01:36,104 --> 00:01:39,810
but when he tried to summon
an image, nothing appeared.

28
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:43,418
Imagine knowing exactly what
your kitchen looks like,

29
00:01:43,458 --> 00:01:46,690
but being unable to see it
when you close your eyes.

30
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:50,850
A report about MX reached the public.

31
00:01:51,270 --> 00:01:55,567
Then people began contacting the
researchers with a surprising message:

32
00:01:55,607 --> 00:01:58,340
“That’s how my mind has always worked.”

33
00:01:59,570 --> 00:02:01,980
These people hadn’t lost
their mental pictures.

34
00:02:02,230 --> 00:02:05,180
As far as they could remember,
they’d never had them.

35
00:02:05,930 --> 00:02:09,980
Researchers gave this
experience a name: aphantasia.

36
00:02:11,010 --> 00:02:14,934
That doesn’t mean people with aphantasia
see nothing with their eyes closed—most

37
00:02:14,974 --> 00:02:17,900
people just see darkness
when they close their eyes.

38
00:02:18,610 --> 00:02:22,020
The difference is whether they
can create a mental picture.

39
00:02:22,730 --> 00:02:26,260
And mental pictures don’t
have to look like photographs.

40
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:31,290
For many people, they’re faint,
fleeting, or difficult to describe.

41
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:34,050
And here is the surprising part.

42
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:39,211
In one early study, most of the people
who reported aphantasia could still see

43
00:02:39,251 --> 00:02:40,890
images while dreaming.

44
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:44,970
Their difficulty was with
creating pictures on purpose.

45
00:02:45,930 --> 00:02:48,120
Here is another way to
feel the difference.

46
00:02:49,160 --> 00:02:52,410
Try counting the windows in your
home without looking around.

47
00:02:52,880 --> 00:02:56,870
You may mentally walk from room
to room and see each window.

48
00:02:57,160 --> 00:03:01,370
Or you might simply know how many there
are, without picturing a single room.

49
00:03:02,030 --> 00:03:06,302
In research, people with aphantasia
were able to do tasks like this,

50
00:03:06,342 --> 00:03:09,473
but they described using
facts, spatial knowledge,

51
00:03:09,513 --> 00:03:12,400
or other strategies instead of pictures.

52
00:03:13,110 --> 00:03:16,270
So if a person says they cannot
see pictures in their head,

53
00:03:16,310 --> 00:03:20,620
can science tell whether they’re really
experiencing something different?

54
00:03:22,510 --> 00:03:25,440
One experiment offers a fascinating clue.

55
00:03:26,280 --> 00:03:30,170
Look at a bright light, and the
pupils in your eyes become smaller.

56
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:31,830
That happens automatically.

57
00:03:33,050 --> 00:03:34,500
You don’t have to think about it.

58
00:03:34,770 --> 00:03:39,800
In a 2022 study, researchers asked
people to look at bright and dark shapes.

59
00:03:40,070 --> 00:03:44,020
Then they took the shapes away and
asked participants to imagine them.

60
00:03:44,530 --> 00:03:49,025
For people who reported having mental
images, imagining something bright made their

61
00:03:49,065 --> 00:03:53,840
pupils shrink a little—almost as though
their eyes were reacting to a real image.

62
00:03:54,250 --> 00:03:59,010
In the small group who reported aphantasia,
researchers didn’t find that same clear

63
00:03:59,050 --> 00:04:00,050
response.

64
00:04:00,650 --> 00:04:04,660
This doesn’t mean an eye test can
tell you exactly what someone thinks.

65
00:04:04,730 --> 00:04:09,597
But it suggests that imagining a picture
can have effects on the body—and that those

66
00:04:09,637 --> 00:04:11,740
effects differ between people.

67
00:04:12,350 --> 00:04:15,580
Another study found something
intriguing about emotion.

68
00:04:16,460 --> 00:04:20,917
When people read frightening stories,
those with aphantasia showed less of a

69
00:04:20,957 --> 00:04:23,970
particular physical fear
response on average.

70
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:29,148
But when they looked at scary photographs,
their responses were comparable to other

71
00:04:29,188 --> 00:04:30,180
people’s.

72
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:33,010
The finding doesn’t mean
they can’t feel fear.

73
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:37,509
It suggests that imagining a scene
and actually seeing one can affect us

74
00:04:37,549 --> 00:04:38,549
differently.

75
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:42,086
Now let’s turn to something
even more familiar:

76
00:04:42,126 --> 00:04:43,810
the voice in your head.

77
00:04:45,740 --> 00:04:50,410
Maybe you silently rehearse what to
say before an important conversation.

78
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:53,890
Maybe you tell yourself,
“Don’t forget the keys.”

79
00:04:54,680 --> 00:04:59,110
Or maybe your thoughts rarely
take the form of words at all.

80
00:04:59,580 --> 00:05:03,450
Psychologists call thinking
in inner words inner speech.

81
00:05:04,370 --> 00:05:09,360
It can be a full sentence, a fragment, or
something that barely feels like a voice.

82
00:05:10,090 --> 00:05:13,740
It isn’t the same as hearing a
sound coming from outside you.

83
00:05:13,890 --> 00:05:18,360
Sometimes you know you’re thinking in words
even though there’s no distinct sound.

84
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:23,608
And this is important: having no mental
pictures and having little inner speech are

85
00:05:23,648 --> 00:05:25,090
not the same thing.

86
00:05:25,670 --> 00:05:28,685
We’re looking at two different
dimensions of experience,

87
00:05:28,725 --> 00:05:30,820
not two names for one condition.

88
00:05:31,150 --> 00:05:34,980
One influential explanation
comes from watching children.

89
00:05:35,670 --> 00:05:38,630
A young child solving a
puzzle might say out loud,

90
00:05:38,670 --> 00:05:39,980
“Not that piece.

91
00:05:40,610 --> 00:05:41,760
This one.”

92
00:05:41,810 --> 00:05:45,040
Over time, some of that
self-talk becomes silent.

93
00:05:45,170 --> 00:05:47,900
But that doesn’t mean
everybody uses it equally.

94
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:50,924
Think about how you plan
a trip, solve a problem,

95
00:05:50,964 --> 00:05:53,250
or rehearse a difficult conversation.

96
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:58,347
Do you talk yourself through the
steps, see situations unfolding,

97
00:05:58,387 --> 00:06:01,470
or simply move from one
decision to the next?

98
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:05,290
Different people may recognize
different parts of that description.

99
00:06:07,320 --> 00:06:12,428
In 2024, researchers compared people who
said they had a lot of inner speech with

100
00:06:12,468 --> 00:06:15,170
people who said they had very little.

101
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:17,830
They gave both groups a series of tasks.

102
00:06:18,310 --> 00:06:22,693
One involved looking at pictures and
deciding whether the names of the objects

103
00:06:22,733 --> 00:06:23,733
rhymed.

104
00:06:24,090 --> 00:06:25,240
Try it yourself.

105
00:06:25,890 --> 00:06:28,720
Imagine a picture of a
sock next to a clock.

106
00:06:29,050 --> 00:06:30,320
Do their names rhyme?

107
00:06:30,850 --> 00:06:34,583
Now imagine doing many of those
decisions, including tricky ones,

108
00:06:34,623 --> 00:06:35,960
without speaking.

109
00:06:36,250 --> 00:06:39,400
The two groups answered
at roughly the same speed.

110
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:44,630
But the group reporting less inner speech
made more mistakes on the rhyming task.

111
00:06:45,480 --> 00:06:50,210
The researchers also asked people to
remember lists of words that sounded alike.

112
00:06:50,850 --> 00:06:54,340
Again, the group with less
inner speech found that harder.

113
00:06:54,790 --> 00:06:57,360
Does that mean those people
are worse at thinking?

114
00:06:58,340 --> 00:06:59,490
No.

115
00:06:59,550 --> 00:07:02,200
On other tasks, the groups
performed similarly.

116
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,591
And when participants spoke words out
loud to help with the memory task,

117
00:07:06,631 --> 00:07:07,990
the gap disappeared.

118
00:07:08,490 --> 00:07:12,160
The lesson isn’t that one
kind of mind is better.

119
00:07:12,730 --> 00:07:17,140
It’s that different people may rely on
different tools to solve the same problem.

120
00:07:18,020 --> 00:07:21,350
And this study doesn’t prove what
is happening in everyone’s head.

121
00:07:21,660 --> 00:07:26,050
The groups were selected because they
reported very different experiences.

122
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:29,470
Researchers then looked for
differences in their performance.

123
00:07:30,230 --> 00:07:33,657
That’s useful evidence, but
it isn’t a population survey,

124
00:07:33,697 --> 00:07:37,880
and it can’t tell us how many
people never hear an inner voice.

125
00:07:39,900 --> 00:07:41,930
But there’s a catch.

126
00:07:42,790 --> 00:07:47,989
Saying you rarely hear words in your mind
is not the same as proving you never have

127
00:07:48,029 --> 00:07:49,200
inner speech.

128
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:52,830
Researchers still debate
where to draw that line.

129
00:07:53,570 --> 00:07:58,320
Even the people who study this disagree
about what “no inner voice” should mean.

130
00:07:58,610 --> 00:08:01,426
Which leads to a much more
uncomfortable question:

131
00:08:01,466 --> 00:08:04,900
Do we actually know what’s
happening in our own heads?

132
00:08:07,340 --> 00:08:09,830
Imagine you’re going
about an ordinary day.

133
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:11,870
Suddenly, a beep sounds.

134
00:08:12,610 --> 00:08:17,502
At that exact moment, you have to notice
what was happening in your mind just before

135
00:08:17,542 --> 00:08:18,542
the beep.

136
00:08:19,070 --> 00:08:20,340
Was there a sentence?

137
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:22,310
A picture?

138
00:08:22,590 --> 00:08:23,570
A feeling?

139
00:08:23,610 --> 00:08:25,820
Or something harder to describe?

140
00:08:29,810 --> 00:08:33,720
Psychologist Russell Hurlburt
has used a method like this.

141
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:37,790
People carry a device that
beeps at unpredictable times.

142
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:42,391
They record what they were experiencing
immediately before each beep,

143
00:08:42,431 --> 00:08:45,810
and later discuss those
moments in careful interviews.

144
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:50,945
In one small study, inner speech appeared
in only about a quarter of all sampled

145
00:08:50,985 --> 00:08:51,985
moments.

146
00:08:52,170 --> 00:08:54,400
Some participants reported it frequently.

147
00:08:55,230 --> 00:08:57,970
Others reported none in the
moments that were sampled.

148
00:08:58,010 --> 00:09:02,380
That does not prove those people
never experienced inner speech.

149
00:09:02,510 --> 00:09:06,880
But it challenges the idea that
everyone is silently talking all day.

150
00:09:07,350 --> 00:09:10,766
The researchers also found
something especially strange:

151
00:09:10,806 --> 00:09:13,440
what they call unsymbolized thinking.

152
00:09:14,150 --> 00:09:17,420
Imagine suddenly knowing
you should call a friend.

153
00:09:17,790 --> 00:09:20,410
There’s no sentence in
your mind saying it.

154
00:09:20,450 --> 00:09:22,140
No picture of a phone.

155
00:09:22,270 --> 00:09:23,800
Just the thought itself.

156
00:09:24,250 --> 00:09:29,420
If that sounds impossible to you, it may
feel completely ordinary to someone else.

157
00:09:30,290 --> 00:09:34,641
Here’s why those interviews matter: the
researchers weren’t just asking people to

158
00:09:34,681 --> 00:09:36,820
summarize how they usually think.

159
00:09:37,730 --> 00:09:40,580
They were asking about
specific, fleeting moments.

160
00:09:41,260 --> 00:09:45,559
That’s a much harder question, and a reminder
that our memories of our own thoughts

161
00:09:45,599 --> 00:09:46,890
can be incomplete.

162
00:09:48,830 --> 00:09:52,020
And this difference can
matter in everyday life.

163
00:09:52,670 --> 00:09:57,531
People with aphantasia sometimes report
that they remember facts about their past

164
00:09:57,571 --> 00:10:00,160
without mentally reliving the scenes.

165
00:10:00,710 --> 00:10:02,370
Think about your last birthday.

166
00:10:04,280 --> 00:10:07,750
Perhaps you can almost see
the room and the faces.

167
00:10:08,200 --> 00:10:12,119
Another person might remember who
was there, what everyone said,

168
00:10:12,159 --> 00:10:15,850
and why it mattered, without
replaying a single image.

169
00:10:16,240 --> 00:10:20,324
Both people remember the event, but their
experiences of remembering can be very

170
00:10:20,364 --> 00:10:21,364
different.

171
00:10:22,100 --> 00:10:26,677
Yet studies have also found that they can
perform just as accurately as other people

172
00:10:26,717 --> 00:10:30,310
on certain visual memory tasks,
using different strategies.

173
00:10:30,560 --> 00:10:33,696
So a missing mental picture
doesn’t mean a missing memory,

174
00:10:33,736 --> 00:10:35,510
or a lack of intelligence.

175
00:10:35,780 --> 00:10:38,130
And the opposite extreme exists too.

176
00:10:38,840 --> 00:10:43,670
Some people describe mental images so
vivid they almost resemble real sight.

177
00:10:44,200 --> 00:10:48,670
Even among people who can visualize,
the experience can vary enormously.

178
00:10:49,100 --> 00:10:54,447
What seems obvious or effortless to one
person may be completely unfamiliar to

179
00:10:54,487 --> 00:10:55,487
another.

180
00:10:57,450 --> 00:10:58,780
Think back to the beginning.

181
00:10:59,690 --> 00:11:00,840
Could you see a face?

182
00:11:01,230 --> 00:11:02,660
Did you hear a sentence?

183
00:11:03,070 --> 00:11:06,800
Were there words,
images, both, or neither?

184
00:11:07,850 --> 00:11:10,220
Those moments are clues, not labels.

185
00:11:10,470 --> 00:11:13,270
They don’t tell you everything
about how your mind works.

186
00:11:13,310 --> 00:11:18,562
But they reveal something easy to forget:
Your own experience of thinking isn’t the

187
00:11:18,602 --> 00:11:20,100
only possible one.

188
00:11:20,480 --> 00:11:25,250
A thought can come as a voice, a
picture, a feeling—or simply as knowing.

189
00:11:25,850 --> 00:11:28,140
And that raises one last question.

190
00:11:28,970 --> 00:11:34,020
Someone close to you might have an
inner world completely unlike yours.

191
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:36,990
Have you ever asked them what it’s like?

